; ITEMSNEW.SCR — Stage 1 of the new armor/shield/substance crafting framework
; (source: "LoFP Crafting_Armor_Shields_Substances.xlsx", designer notes 2026-08-29).
;
; First pass (items 9100-9110) covered SHIELDS, as a proof of the pattern.
; Second pass (9111-9144) covers the Metal Armors and Leather & Cloth Armors
; sheets: head/torso/hands/feet pieces. Third pass (9145) is a single Thigh
; Guards item, the Misc. Items sheet's Shoulder Guards' structural twin (see
; below). Stage 1 (items), Stage 2 (combat.go per-body-part DR), and Stage 3
; (doCraft skill routing, incl. the PARAMETER2 skill-level values below) are
; all done as of this pass. Only the REINFORCE command (two-crafter combo
; items) and breakage remain as deliberate follow-ons.
;
; Design: each shield SHAPE reuses the existing NOUN 182 "shield" (buckler and
; main-gauche keep their own existing single-word nouns, 37 and 317) plus a new
; ItemDef-level ADJECTIVE/ADJECTIVE2 (see gameworld.ItemDef.Adjective, mirrors
; MonsterDef.Adjective/FormatMonsterName) so it displays as "round shield" /
; "large round shield" rather than needing an invented hyphenated noun.
; ADJECTIVE is a FIXED part of the archetype's identity, distinct from the
; three per-instance Adj1-3 slots (crafted-in material, dye, encrusted gems,
; applied later via WORK/REINFORCE/ENCRUST) — it's what CRAFTABLE items need in
; place of the adjective a room ITEM placement or a StoreItem entry would
; otherwise supply for a purchased/placed instance.
;
; Each shape (except main-gauche) gets TWO archetypes sharing noun+adjective: a
; WOOD tier (Wood Lore/fletcher) and a HARDMETAL tier (Weaponsmithing/forge).
; They don't collide despite sharing a display name because doCraft already
; gates on the room's workshop modifier before skill — whichever workshop
; you're standing in determines which same-named archetype you actually reach
; (see getItemNounName in look.go, which composes ADJECTIVE/ADJECTIVE2 into
; the noun for every command that targets an item by name — used directly by
; doCraft's listing/matching in crafting.go). The spreadsheet's third
; "metal-reinforced" tier is intentionally NOT a third archetype — it's a
; future REINFORCE <shield> WITH <metal> command upgrading the wood tier's
; instance in place (same idiom as ENCRUST/INLAY in crafting.go's
; doGemAdorn), so it doesn't need its own item number.
;
; No material adjective (wooden/steel/iron/...) is baked into any archetype —
; that's chosen at craft time via WORK <material>, same as metal weapons today.
;
; PARAMETER2 is the crafting skill-level requirement for every ARMOR/SHIELD
; item below (see the comment in doCraft, crafting.go) — PARAMETER1 on these
; is already taken by the DR%/DefBonus stat, so PARAMETER2 carries the level
; instead, matching every pre-existing CRAFTABLE ARMOR item in the original
; 1990s data (all of which already have an explicit PARAMETER2 unrelated to
; Weight). No item below is CRAFTABLE with a level of 0 — every one requires
; at least level 1.
;
; Level curve (revised 2026-09-01 after the user caught the first pass
; clustering everything at 1-11): checked the real distribution of every
; pre-existing CRAFTABLE item's skill level — Dyeing/Weaving spans 1-40 (91
; items), Weaponsmithing/Wood weapons span 1-45 (36 items), Wood Lore
; non-weapon items span 1-30. The first pass only used 1-11, badly
; undershooting the game's actual scale, so a level-16 weaver blew past every
; new item instantly. Rebuilt using the spreadsheet's own DR%/DefBonus as the
; complexity driver instead of Weight (Weight was never the right proxy — see
; the Stage 3 PARAMETER2 discovery note in the project memory), scaled so the
; heaviest pieces (Full Plate, Tower Shield) land in the low-20s alongside
; comparable high-end weapons/robes, and a torso piece costs more than an
; accessory piece (gauntlets/boots/cap) at the same DR% — more material, more
; construction. The buckler wood tier stays at the user's explicit level 2
; anchor; everything else (including the wood kite shield, previously 5) was
; rescaled to keep proper relative spacing above and below that anchor. Still
; expect further tuning — this is a considered pass, not a final one.
;
; NOUNDEF/ADJDEF must stay at true top level (before any INUMBER block) — the
; parser's item sub-loop doesn't recognize them as block terminators. round
; (265), large (178), and tower (1011) already exist from the original 1990s
; data; only "kite" is new.
ADJDEF 9100 kite
;
; Second-pass (armor) additions. Nearly every word needed already existed in
; the original 1990s data (helm, basinet, cap, hat, skull, plate, mail, scale,
; chain, boots, gloves, gauntlets, leather, hide, boiled, supple, cloth,
; diadem, circlet) — these four are the only genuinely new ones. "brigandine"
; needs both forms: a NOUN (the torso piece stands alone, e.g. "a brigandine")
; and an ADJECTIVE (for "brigandine gauntlets"/"brigandine boots"), same as
; "mail"/"scale"/"chain" already exist as both in the original data.
NOUNDEF 9101 barbute
NOUNDEF 9102 headdress
NOUNDEF 9103 gambeson
NOUNDEF 9104 brigandine
ADJDEF 9105 kettle
ADJDEF 9106 layered
ADJDEF 9107 brigandine
;
; Buckler, wood tier — smallest shield, Wood Lore/fletcher crafted. No shape
; adjective needed; "buckler" already names the shape.
